Relicensed from MIT on 2026-08-18. The project has had exactly one author and
no outside contributors, so the change required no permission from anybody and
nothing already distributed under MIT is withdrawn: a copy obtained under MIT
stays under MIT. Apache 2.0 was chosen for the two things MIT does not say.

It grants a patent licence explicitly, and terminates that grant for anybody who
sues the project over patents. A marketplace that settles payments on chain is a
more attractive target for that than most software, and MIT leaves the question
open rather than answering it.

It also requires modified files to be marked. This codebase is deliberately full
of comments explaining why a guard exists and what it was found to be wrong
about, and a fork that silently removes one of those guards while keeping the
comment would be a genuine hazard to whoever runs it.
